Your wazuh-logtest output says:
Phase 1: Completed pre-decoding
full event: 'Oct 5 22:16:09 localhost sshd-session[5647]: Failed password...'

Phase 2: Completed decoding.
No decoder matched.

So the event is reaching the Wazuh analysis engine, but no decoder recognizes this RHEL 10 sshd-session format. The standard Wazuh SSH example uses sshd, while your RHEL 10 log uses sshd-session. Wazuh's documentation confirms that decoders are responsible for extracting fields before rules can generate alerts. Wazuh Documentation
Also, your event is from October 5, while today is October 7, 2026. That's fine for testing the decoder, but after we create the decoder we'll generate a fresh October 7 event and verify the whole pipeline.
Today we're going to build the SSH decoder


We'll make Wazuh understand:
sshd-session[5647]: Failed password for invalid user wronguser from 192.168.95.129 port 55926 ssh2

and extract:
username = wronguser
source IP = 192.168.95.129
source port = 55926

Then we'll create a custom rule that produces an alert.
Wazuh recommends putting custom decoders in:
/var/ossec/etc/decoders/

rather than modifying the built-in ruleset.

MAIN STEPSSSSSSS ##########################################################################################################################################################

Step 1 — Create the custom decoder
On your Ubuntu Wazuh server, run:
sudo nano /var/ossec/etc/decoders/local_decoder.xml

If the file already contains decoders, don't delete them.
Add this before the final </decoder>/end of the file as appropriate. If it's empty, use this complete content:
<decoder name="rhel-sshd-session">
    <prematch>sshd-session</prematch>
</decoder>

<decoder name="rhel-sshd-session-failed">
    <parent>rhel-sshd-session</parent>
    <regex>Failed password for invalid user (\S+) from (\S+) port (\d+)</regex>
    <order>dstuser,srcip,srcport</order>
</decoder>

What this does
The first decoder recognizes:
sshd-session

The child decoder extracts:
wronguser
192.168.95.129
55926



